WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.442
Your slowest requests just got slower.

2
00:00:05.442 --> 00:00:08.793
Is the garbage collector stopping your threads?

3
00:00:08.793 --> 00:00:13.569
Turn on -Xlog:gc, and the JVM prints one line for every collection.

4
00:00:13.733 --> 00:00:17.261
Read one line as an event on a timeline.

5
00:00:17.261 --> 00:00:22.024
This one happened 0.128 seconds after the JVM started.

6
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:25.066
It's collection number zero, a young pause.

7
00:00:25.066 --> 00:00:28.066
Pause means every application thread stopped.

8
00:00:28.066 --> 00:00:31.799
Normal means G1 cleaned only the young part of the heap.

9
00:00:31.867 --> 00:00:33.848
The cause: eden was full.

10
00:00:33.848 --> 00:00:38.920
The heap went from 14 megabytes used to 1, out of 256 committed.

11
00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:43.809
And 12.723 milliseconds is how long your threads stood still.

12
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:49.124
Here are eight real lines from a busier G1 run, under a second of time.

13
00:00:49.124 --> 00:00:51.163
Which ones stopped your threads?

14
00:00:52.733 --> 00:00:57.733
A pause line has Pause right after the GC number, and ends in milliseconds.

15
00:00:57.733 --> 00:01:00.000
Each one cuts across every thread.

16
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:03.103
Concurrent Mark Cycle ends in milliseconds too.

17
00:01:03.103 --> 00:01:06.315
But it ran beside your threads, not instead of them.

18
00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:08.355
Three more pauses follow.

19
00:01:08.355 --> 00:01:15.933
So six of the eight lines are pauses, and the longest is the full GC at the end: almost 47 milliseconds.

20
00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.545
Two words deserve a closer look: Full, and Evacuation Failure.

21
00:01:20.545 --> 00:01:22.997
A healthy G1 service shows neither.

22
00:01:23.867 --> 00:01:25.908
Now, how big is that heap?

23
00:01:25.908 --> 00:01:29.598
In a container, the JVM reads the memory limit.

24
00:01:29.598 --> 00:01:33.209
With no heap flags, the max heap is 25% of it.

25
00:01:33.333 --> 00:01:37.417
So a pod with a 1 GiB limit gets a 256 MB heap.

26
00:01:37.417 --> 00:01:40.024
Why leave three quarters free?

27
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.702
Because the heap isn't the whole process.

28
00:01:43.702 --> 00:01:51.019
Metaspace, the code cache, a stack per thread, the GC's own bookkeeping and direct buffers all live outside it.

29
00:01:51.133 --> 00:01:59.588
With MaxRAMPercentage=75, the same pod gets 768 MB, and the rest still fits.

30
00:01:59.733 --> 00:02:02.387
Now set -Xmx1g, the whole limit.

31
00:02:02.387 --> 00:02:05.123
What happens once the heap fills?

32
00:02:07.067 --> 00:02:12.331
Everything else goes past the limit, and the kernel kills the process.

33
00:02:12.331 --> 00:02:13.383
Exit code 137.

34
00:02:13.383 --> 00:02:15.940
No OutOfMemoryError, no heap dump.

35
00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:22.050
In production, log to rotating files, and size the heap with a percentage.

36
00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:26.733
These are JVM flags, the same for a Java service and a Kotlin one.

37
00:02:27.533 --> 00:02:28.258
The rule.

38
00:02:28.258 --> 00:02:33.006
A line with Pause after the GC number stopped your threads.

39
00:02:33.006 --> 00:02:37.111
Size the heap as 50 to 75% of the limit, never 100.

40
00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.130
Quick check.

41
00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:41.930
A pod with a 4 GiB limit runs with no heap flags.

42
00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:43.791
How big is the max heap?

43
00:02:46.133 --> 00:02:48.295
1 GiB: a quarter of the limit.
